## Iteration 4 — 2026-10-03 19:28

### Completed
Item 03, objects, sugar and utilities: **SOLVED**.
- **Te

Item 03 is solved: `objects.py`, `sugar.py` and `utilities.py` now exist, and all 197 tests of `tests/diff/utilities-battery.scm` pass against their frozen Chez outputs. The gate passes (379 tests, 36 s). I marked the item `[x]`, added the Iteration 4 entry to PROGRESS.md and set the count to 4/18. 14 items remain, so no `LOOP_COMPLETE`.

- **Tests first:** I wrote `python/tests/test_utilities.py` before any of the three modules existed. It has one case per battery test, plus a check that the case names match the battery's list exactly. Run against the empty package, it failed at import, so every case failed.
- **The three modules:**
  - `objects.py` uses the object design chosen in item 01. A message costs about 135 ns, or 247 ns when passed on to a parent once.
  - `sugar.py` turns each of the 22 macros into a function. `stochastic-if*` draws exactly one `(random 1.0)`, before the probability is evaluated.
  - `utilities.py` has one function per definition in `utilities.ss`. A test checks that every Scheme definition has its Python counterpart.
- **Other changes:** the name mapping moved into the package as `metacat/names.py`. `chez.py` gained `ExactComplex`, `make_rectangular`, `make_vector`, `string_to_number` and `atan`, because coordinates like `(coord 3 4)` are exact `3+4i` in Chez, which Python's `complex` can't hold.
- **Checking the tests have teeth:** all 197 cases passed on the first run, which looked suspicious, so I tried 40 deliberate bugs (wrong order of calls, wrong comparisons, truthiness slips and so on). 39 were caught. The one that got through is equivalent to the original, so no test can tell them apart. One bug, `prob?` using `>=`, was only caught after I added a small extra Chez battery (`utilities-extra-battery.scm`, 4 tests) for the case where the probability equals the draw. Unlike the 197, those 4 tests were written after the code, and PROGRESS.md says so.
- **A finding for later items:** the plan assumed the model never tells strings from symbols. The graphics files and `rules.ss:269` do. I logged it in `docs/anomalies_and_quirks.md` as open, along with two Python traps: hyphenated strings like `"invalid-message-indicator"` aren't automatically shared objects, and the exact-complex gap above.

Still to do in later items: arithmetic on exact complex coordinates (the graphics need it), and `ask`, which only handles symbols and numbers on one line rather than a full Scheme reader; no run uses it.

